  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "If \\(3=k\\cdot2^r\\) and \\(15=k\\cdot4^r,\\) then \\(r=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\log_2 5\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\log_5 2\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\log_{10}5\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\log_2 5\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac52\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Both equations contain the same factor \\(k\\), so division will eliminate it. Since \\(2^r>0\\), the first equation gives \\(k>0\\), making this division valid. Dividing the second equation by the first gives\n\\[\n5=\\frac{15}{3}\n=\\frac{k4^r}{k2^r}\n=\\frac{2^{2r}}{2^r}\n=2^r.\n\\]\nBy definition, \\(\\log_2 5\\) is the exponent to which \\(2\\) must be raised to obtain \\(5\\). This value is feasible: with \\(k=3/5\\), the two original right-hand sides are \\(3\\) and \\(15\\). Therefore \\(r=\\log_2 5\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
